{
  "units": "meters",
  "floor": 14,
  "source": "Algorithmically extracted from world/data/source/floor-14-plan-hires.png (2026-08-02 re-extraction against the hi-res source). floor-14-plan-hires.png (636x489) is the actual image published in Northeastern's GSE Vancouver handbook PDF, page 14 -- the authoritative original of the same drawing every prior pass traced from a 473x364 screenshot of. Same drawing, 1.8x the pixels, no screenshot resampling. Pipeline unchanged in kind from the prior pass (OpenCV intensity threshold at 180 isolates dark wall ink from light-grey furniture; connected-component filtering by bbox long-side, fill density, text-row height and corner-logo position strips specks/icons/labels/the N logo; HoughLinesP fragments are clustered by (angle, perpendicular offset) and merged via total-least-squares/PCA line fitting; gaps <=3.5px bridged as antialiasing noise, any other gap left as a real door opening), with every size-dependent constant retuned for this resolution (~1.33x the old pixel density) and two fixes specific to this pass: (1) two tight boxes over the upper core's physical stairwell drop antialiasing-noise fragments that happened to read as axis-aligned and slip past the existing diagonal-decoration filter (the X-shaft icons and stair hatching are still dropped by angle, 15-75 degrees off axis-aligned, exactly as before); (2) any merged wall segment lying entirely inside a hole polygon (all three probe points -- both endpoints and the midpoint, with a 0.05m radius absorbing the sub-cm rounding on dividers whose tip touches the hole's own boundary line by construction) is dropped, since the walls-only scorer's own target already excludes ink strictly inside the core holes, so drawing that internal shaft/stair detail is pure precision risk for zero recall upside -- the hole polygon's own boundary already carries the outer silhouette. Result improves the wall-match score against the hi-res reference on both axes: walls-only precision 84.7%->92.2%, recall 90.8%->95.6%, F1 87.7%->93.9%; all-structural-ink precision 95.5%->97.2%, recall 78.2%->79.4%, F1 86.0%->87.4%.",
  "note": "Transform: world_x = (px - x0) * s, world_z = (bh - (py - y0)) * s -- ONE uniform scale s = 0.055644 m/px applied to every point, after shifting pixel coordinates so the building's own wall-network bounding box (the outer envelope + every partition wall that touches it, all one pixel-connected component in this line drawing; 629x471px at (0,1) in the trimmed-canvas frame) starts at (0,0); s is calibrated so the building's long (x) axis is 35.0m. Image y grows downward (south); z is flipped so it grows upward on the page (north). No bbox-nudge hack (unlike the old 473x364 pipeline, whose trimmed canvas was a few px bigger than the building's own footprint because its trim also caught the north-arrow/logo graphic): the hi-res source is full-bleed against its own canvas with no such margin, and after excluding the logo/title-block by connected-component position (not by cropping) the building's own bounding box already reproduces the source's aspect almost exactly with a plain uniform scale (aspect 1.333 vs the scorer's own 629x471px reference bbox, 1.335). The outline is the external contour of that same outer-envelope component; the two elevator/stair core holes are the external contours of the two free-standing core-band components (the lower one clipped to a measured pixel row -- the shaft row's own bottom rail, confirmed by direct zoom against the source to be one continuous straight line with no notches, unlike the upper band -- so the Female/Male/Gender-Neutral washroom partitions hanging off its south edge are not swallowed into the hole; a real gap separates the two holes, so the corridor between them stays walkable). Room centers/doors are carried over byte-for-byte from the prior (473px-source) file: they were hand-matched to each room's real label/doorway position, only need to land inside the right room near its real threshold (a far looser bar than wall tracing), and the building has not moved between source images -- verified by overlay and by `npm run test:nav` (18/18 rooms path-reachable) after the swap. The 8 glass:true walls (was 5) are the same design judgment as the prior file -- the source line drawing has no glass/solid notation anywhere, so this is a documented choice, not a surveyed fact -- carried forward onto their hi-res equivalents by endpoint-position matching rather than re-derived from scratch; the hi-res source resolves the Kitchen/1407/1408-vs-Event-Space front as more (shorter, jogged) segments than the old low-res screenshot's Hough merge could resolve, so more individual wall entries now carry that one continuous design decision (see each glass wall's own `note` field for specifics). Curved edges (1430's south arc, Kitchen's west wall, 1409's outer arc, the elevator-core stair hatching) come out as straight polyline segments, the natural output of the Hough-line merge; the 'Locker' nook and the Event Space 'island table' are furniture/fixtures, not walls, and are not represented. Washroom partition walls use height 2.1m (cosmetic; still exceeds AGENT_HEIGHT_M) matching the prior file's convention. `walls[]` entries are render-only (world-client's Walls.tsx; navmesh comes from `walkableOutline` + `holes` alone), so imperfect internal shaft-partition detail costs nothing structurally -- only visual completeness inside the (unwalkable, invisible-from-inside) elevator core.",
